Code advice test 8/10: My OnStartUp config parser silently ignores unknown mission-file parameters. Is that OK? Explain how MOOS-IvP AppCasting apps normally handle unrecognized config lines and why preserving/reporting the original line helps. Cite sources.

Silently ignoring unknown mission-file parameters is not recommended in the MOOS-IvP architecture. Doing so makes it difficult for a user to identify simple typos or misconfigurations, as the app will appear to be running normally while ignoring the user's intended settings
1
.
How AppCasting Apps Handle Unrecognized Lines
Standard AppCastingMOOSApp implementations follow a specific pattern in OnStartUp() to ensure all configuration lines are accounted for. Instead of a simple "if/else" block that ignores the final "else" case, you should use reportUnhandledConfigWarning()
1
2
.
The recommended pattern is to iterate through the list of configuration parameters and, if a parameter does not match any of your expected keys, pass the original raw configuration line to this superclass function
2
:
bool YourMOOSApp::OnStartUp() {
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp(); // 1. Call superclass

  STRING_LIST sParams;
  m_MissionReader.GetConfiguration(GetAppName(), sParams);

  for (string orig_line : sParams) {
    string line = orig_line;
    string param = toupper(MOOSChomp(line, "="));
    string value = line;

    if (param == "FOO") {
      // handle FOO...
    } else if (param == "BAR") {
      // handle BAR...
    } else {
      // 2. Report unrecognized lines to the superclass
      reportUnhandledConfigWarning(orig_line);
    }
  }
  return true;
}

Why Preserving and Reporting the Original Line Matters
Filtering Superclass Parameters: The reportUnhandledConfigWarning() function is intelligent. Before it actually posts a warning, it checks if the parameter was something handled by the base classes (such as AppTick, CommsTick, or MAX_APPCAST_EVENTS)
1
3
. This ensures you don't generate "unknown parameter" warnings for standard MOOS-IvP infrastructure variables
1
.
Catching Misspellings: By reporting the line in the "else" case, you immediately catch errors like speed = 2.0 vs. sped = 2.0.
Visual Diagnostics (The "Green Highlight"): When an app posts a configuration warning, its name and status will be rendered in green in tools like uMAC, uMACView, and pMarineViewer
4
. This allows an operator to instantly see that an application is "MALCONFIG" or has an unresolved setup issue without having to dig through raw terminal logs
4
5
.
Audit Trail: The appcast report will explicitly list the offending line

